{
  "type": "Application",
  "analysis": "Paper A applies the standardized cryo-CLEM sample preparation and RSV infection protocols established in Paper B to experimentally validate its novel montage cryo-ET workflow. By leveraging the specific cell lines and viral models detailed in Paper B, the authors demonstrate the practical utility and scalability of their new imaging pipeline for capturing large-scale, context-rich 3D architectures of infected cells."
}

{
  "type": "Background",
  "analysis": "Paper A addresses field-of-view and radiation damage limitations in cryo-ET via montage acquisition, whereas Paper B tackles cryo-transfer and correlation bottlenecks through an integrated light/FIB-SEM microscope. Paper A cites Paper B to contextualize contemporary advancements in cryo-CLEM hardware that enable precise, low-risk cellular targeting during FIB-milling, providing necessary methodological background for the correlative step in A's workflow without establishing a direct technical dependency."
}

{
  "type": "Background",
  "analysis": "Paper A cites Paper B to contextualize the 'Montage ET' imaging strategy as a pivotal advancement for overcoming the resolution-versus-field-of-view trade-off in cryo-ET. It specifically references Paper B's optimized tiling, global translational dose distribution, and automated stitching workflow to illustrate how large-scale 3D cellular imaging is achieved, while acknowledging the method's inherent costs such as elongated acquisition times."
}

{
  "type": "Alternative",
  "analysis": "Paper A cites Paper B to position its parallel tilt-series acquisition strategy as a distinct alternative to the montage tomography approach detailed in Paper B. While both methods utilize beam image shifts to overcome traditional cryoET limitations, Paper B focuses on expanding the field of view through tiled stitching and global dose distribution, whereas Paper A leverages image shifts to simultaneously collect multiple independent tilt series on a single lamella, thereby bypassing the stitching complexities, overlap management, and wasted tracking space inherent in montage workflows."
}

{
  "type": "Background",
  "analysis": "Paper A cites Paper B to exemplify recent methodological advances in cryo-ET acquisition, specifically illustrating how montage tomography and automated stitching strategies overcome traditional field-of-view limitations. The review references Paper B’s workflow—including its global translational dose-distribution approach and seamless CLEM/FIB-milling integration—to contextualize the current state-of-the-art in large-scale, high-resolution cellular imaging. This citation provides technical background and evidence for the technological improvements driving the transformation of in situ structural cell biology."
}

{
  "type": "Background",
  "analysis": "Paper B establishes the fundamental physical principles of liquid-liquid phase separation, demonstrating its dependence on intermolecular interactions and macromolecular crowding across diverse proteins. Paper A cites this work to provide theoretical context and motivation for investigating how specific sequence features govern phase separation propensity under more physiologically relevant, dilute conditions, contrasting the universal crowding-driven view with sequence-selective mechanisms."
}

{
  "type": "Foundation",
  "analysis": "Paper B provides the foundational experimental evidence and conceptual shift that intracellular diffusion is scale-dependent, with unobstructed motion at short ranges giving way to hindrance by immobile structural obstacles at longer scales. Paper A builds directly upon this framework by developing a multiscale porous media model that mathematically formalizes these hierarchical nano- and micro-scale effects, identifying the specific physical mechanisms (tortuosity and porous hydrodynamic drag) responsible for the long-range diffusivity reductions observed in Paper B."
}

{
  "type": "Foundation",
  "analysis": "Paper A builds directly upon the foundational LLPS frameworks and synthetic cell paradigms established in Paper B, expanding the earlier review into a comprehensive taxonomy that distinguishes coacervates as either artificial organelles or cytosol templates. It leverages Paper B’s core concepts—such as associative/segregative phase separation, stimuli-responsive dynamics, and cytoskeleton-driven division—to systematically analyze updated physicochemical properties, advanced functionalities, and emerging challenges. By integrating recent advances since 2019, Paper A extends the theoretical backbone of Paper B while explicitly addressing its noted limitations regarding spatial control and metabolic integration."
}
